 Since these vectors are pairwise orthogonal, we know that any two vectors chosen will be orthogonal (perpendicular) in 3-dimensional space.  This means that the dot product of each pair of vectors is $0.$
We have six pairs to consider here, but due to the symmetry of the problem, we only need to consider three of them.  We can write out the equations from three of these:
\begin{align*}
\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ - 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}&=0,\\
\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ - 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} a \\ 1 \\ c \end{pmatrix}&=0,\\
\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} a \\ 1 \\ c \end{pmatrix}&=0.
\end{align*}
Evaluating these dot products, we find the following three equations:
\begin{align*}
2 - 4 + 2 &= 0,\\
a - 1 + 2c &= 0,\\
2a + 4 + c &= 0.
\end{align*}
However, we see that the first equation is just $0=0.$  This means that it will never be true, regardless of the values of $a$ and $c.$  We can ignore it.  Focusing on the second and third equations, we can solve this system to find $a$ and $c.$
\begin{align*}
a + 2c &= 1,\\
2a + c &= -4.
\end{align*}
Multiplying the top equation by $2,$ we find that $2a + 4c = 2.$  Subtracting the bottom equation from this, we get $3c = 6,$ so $c=2.$  Plugging this value of $c$ into the second equation, we find that $a + 2(2) =1,$ so $a=-3.$
Thus, the answer is $\boxed{(-3,2)}.$</think> <answer> $(-3,2)$</answer>

